# Clean code

- Functions do one thing and fit on screen (~30 lines max). If you need a comment to explain a section, extract it into a named function.
- Name variables and functions by intent: `getUsersByRole`, not `getData`; `isEligible`, not `flag`.
- No magic numbers or strings — extract to named constants with a descriptive name.
- Avoid negated booleans: `isEnabled` not `isNotDisabled`, `hasAccess` not `lacksNoAccess`.
- Dead code gets deleted, not commented out. Version control is the archive.
- Prefer early returns over deep nesting — flatten the happy path.
- DRY applies to knowledge, not syntax. Two identical lines serving different purposes are not duplication; two divergent blocks encoding the same business rule are.
- Keep function signatures narrow: 3 parameters or fewer. Beyond that, group into an options object.
- Avoid boolean parameters that silently change behavior — use separate functions or an options object with named fields.
- Write code that reads top-to-bottom: callers above callees, public above private, high-level above detail.
